The conduct relied upon includes, among other matters:
mass arrest and detention in connection with political protest and expression;
prolonged pre-trial detention resulting from restrictive national-security bail provisions;
imprisonment for peaceful political expression, journalism, political organisation and participation;
the use of national-security and sedition legislation against conduct protected under international human-rights law;
restrictions upon access to legal representation, trial by jury and other procedural safeguards;
serious mistreatment of persons in custody, including allegations of torture, sexual violence and prolonged solitary confinement;
coercive measures affecting children and young persons identified through political activity;
the use of administrative and correctional measures to impose additional restrictions upon persons convicted of national-security offences; and
the programme of transnational repression directed against members of the same political population after they left Hong Kong, including arrest warrants, monetary bounties, surveillance, intelligence-gathering, financial restrictions and reprisals against family members.
These measures are alleged to constitute interconnected components of a continuing course of conduct rather than unrelated incidents occurring independently of one another.

The available information further warrants examination of alleged torture committed against persons in custody or under the control of State authorities.
Relevant information includes:
severe beatings and injuries reported among persons detained at the San Uk Ling Holding Centre and other detention facilities;
allegations that detainees were subjected to treatment deliberately calculated to inflict serious physical suffering while avoiding visible evidence of injury;
prolonged solitary confinement of certain political detainees, including periods substantially exceeding the 15-day threshold identified in the Nelson Mandela Rules as prolonged solitary confinement; and
other physical and psychological abuse described in Parts 3, 8 and 9 and the accompanying exhibits.

Available information further contains serious allegations of rape and other forms of sexual violence in custodial settings.
These include:
the complaint made by the woman publicly referred to as Ms X, who alleged that she was raped by multiple police officers while in custody, together with the subsequent investigative and prosecutorial conduct directed against her described in Part 3;
reported sexual assaults and forced strip-searches associated with the San Uk Ling Holding Centre;
accounts of sexual violence against young persons in custodial institutions; and
the assault at Pik Uk Correctional Institution in which a correctional officer, acting together with inmates, penetrated an 18-year-old remand prisoner with a wooden stick, causing permanent injury. (See EXHIBITS C34 &amp; C35)

The information also warrants examination of conduct potentially falling within Article 7(1)(k), including acts alleged intentionally to have caused great suffering or serious injury to mental or physical health.
Relevant conduct includes:
the disproportionate and potentially lethal use of force against civilians during the 2019 protests, including live ammunition, close-range shootings and the use of chemical agents in enclosed or densely populated areas;
serious interference with access to medical treatment for injured or detained persons;
coercive custodial and post-custodial measures capable of causing serious physical or psychological harm;
the cumulative imposition of severe financial, civil and professional restrictions against political dissidents residing overseas; and
coercive measures directed against family members for the purpose of isolating, punishing or exerting pressure upon persons targeted for their political activities.
These acts support an additional characterisation under Article 7(1)(k) where the evidence establishes the required gravity and resulting suffering.

The cases described above span markedly different forms of activity:
participation in an opposition primary election;
legislative strategy;
journalism and political commentary;
annual commemoration of the Tiananmen Square crackdown;
advocacy for political reform in China;
children's books containing political allegory;
low-reach social-media posts;
courtroom expressions of solidarity;
preparation of commemorative displays; and
planned symbolic protest.

In September 2025, the Committee for Freedom in Hong Kong Foundation ("CFHK") published a report concerning the treatment of political prisoners based upon interviews with former detainees and publicly available information. (See EXHIBIT C14)
Its principal findings included:
prolonged solitary confinement;
physical and psychological abuse;
medical neglect; and
coercive political indoctrination.

The broader system includes:
prolonged pre-trial detention;
arbitrary detention identified by United Nations mechanisms;
imprisonment of minors;
prolonged isolation;
physical and psychological abuse;
allegations of organised sexual violence;
a case of severe penetrative sexual violence involving a correctional officer;
political indoctrination and coercive rehabilitation;
additional barriers to early release;
restrictions upon legal and religious visits; and
continuing surveillance, employment restrictions and pressure to provide intelligence after release.

Among other matters, it recorded that:
Owen Chow had been detained for 761 days before trial;
he had been subjected to prolonged solitary confinement;
approximately 74 per cent of national-security defendants within the material examined had been denied bail under the reverse presumption contained in Article 42 of the HKNSL;
the persons subjected to the national-security bail regime included children, some of whom remained detained for more than a year;
the designation of national-security judges by the Chief Executive from a non-public list raised serious concerns regarding judicial independence; and
the removal of trial by jury and concentration of national-security cases before designated judges were incompatible with the guarantees of an independent and impartial tribunal under Article 14(1) of the ICCPR.
(See EXHIBIT B10)

By this stage, the authorities had repeatedly been placed on explicit notice that United Nations mechanisms considered:
national-security detention arbitrary in identified cases;
the reverse bail regime incompatible with international standards;
prolonged solitary confinement capable of producing severe and irreversible harm;
restrictions upon judicial independence incompatible with Article 14 of the ICCPR;
the prosecution of protected political activity incompatible with fundamental civil and political rights; and
allegations of torture and custodial abuse to require effective investigation.

International criminal jurisprudence has repeatedly recognised that crimes against humanity may be committed through imprisonment, persecution, discriminatory legal systems, detention institutions and the organised use of State or quasi-State power against civilian populations.
The most relevant authorities concern five issues central to this report:
territorial jurisdiction over transboundary crimes;
imprisonment and severe deprivation of liberty;
persecution through deprivation of fundamental rights;
the use of legal, judicial and detention institutions as instruments of an attack; and
the fact that conduct presented domestically as law enforcement remains capable of constituting crimes against humanity.

These functions correspond to the institutional pattern described throughout this report:
police and security agencies identify, investigate and arrest targeted persons;
prosecutors invoke national-security and sedition legislation against political activity;
statutory provisions impose differentiated bail, detention and procedural rules;
designated courts adjudicate the resulting prosecutions;
correctional institutions implement imprisonment and additional restrictions;
executive authorities maintain, defend and expand the system; and
the same security apparatus pursues members of the targeted political population after they leave Hong Kong.
